\f3\b\fs56 NSTimeZone Class Cluster\

\fs70 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li200\fc0\cf0 Class Cluster Description\

\fs14 \

\f0\b0\fs28\li1500 From the NSTimeZone class cluster, you can obtain immutable objects that represent time zones. Time zone are split at lines of longitude and are split latitudinally at political boundaries. For example, US/Pacific and Canada/Pacific are two time zone regions. They are roughly the same time zone, but they differ when Daylight Savings Time is in effect. \

\fs14 \

\fs28 The abstract class, NSTimeZone, represents knowledge about a geopolitical time zone region (such as ªUS/Pacificº or ªCanada/Pacificº). An abstract public subclass of NSTimeZone, NSTimeZoneDetail, provides the specific details about a time zone: its colloquial abbreviation (such as ªPSTº), its offset from Greenwich Mean Time (GMT), and whether it is a Daylight Savings Time time zone.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSTimeZone objects are used in conjunction with NSDate objects. NSDate objects represent dates as 
\i universal time
\i0 . Universal time treats a date and time value as identical in, for instance, Redwood City and New York City. There is no provision for locale and, consequently, for time zone. Provision for locale is critical for string representations and other expressions of conventional dates and times. NSTimeZone expands the temporal value of date objects so that they reflect locale information related to time zones. \

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSTimeZone objects are referred to simply as 
\i time zone objects
\i0 . Because of the nature of class clusters, time zone objects returned by this class are 
\i not
\i0  instances of that abstract class (or of NSTimeZoneDetail) but of one of their private subclasses. Although a time zone object's class is private, its interface is public, as declared by the abstract superclass, NSTimeZone. (See ªClass Clustersº in the introduction to the Foundation Kit for more information on class clusters and creating subclasses within a cluster.)\

\fs14 \

\fs28 You use the class methods 
\b defaultTimeZone
\b0 , 
\b localTimeZone
\b0 , 
\b timeZoneWithName:
\b0 , 
\b timeZoneWithAbbreviation:
\b0 , and 
\b timeZoneForSecondsFromGMT:
\b0  to get suitable time zone objects. The instance method 
\b timeZoneDetailForDate:
\b0  returns the time-zone detail object for a specific date.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSTimeZoneDetail doesn't itself provide any class methods for obtaining time zone objects. It exists mainly as a refinement of NSTimeZone. It does, however, provide accessor methods for obtaining abbreviations and GMT offsets and for determining whether the time zone is a Daylight Savings Time time zone.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Subclassing NSTimeZone or NSTimeZoneDetail is not recommended.\

\fs14 \

